<!--  SECTION 6 — KEY FINDINGS & POLICY IMPLICATIONS                       -->
<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="section" id="sec-findings">
  <div class="section-header">
    <div class="section-num">6</div>
    <div>
      <h2>Key Findings &amp; Policy Implications</h2>
      <div class="objective">Executive-ready synthesis of the most actionable insights for energy policymakers.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <ul class="finding-list">
    <li>
      <div class="finding-num">1</div>
      <div class="finding-body">
        <strong>Subsidies deliver a 79.5% price reduction — but volatility protection is the undervalued dividend.</strong>
        <p>High-subsidy countries average $0.71/L vs $3.45/L in Low-subsidy markets. More importantly, subsidy regimes reduce cross-country price volatility by 5–6× in every year. During the 2022 energy crisis, Low-subsidy market std dev hit $1.059/L while High-subsidy markets held at $0.177/L. Policymakers designing subsidy wind-downs must build transition mechanisms (targeted cash transfers, indexed price corridors) to prevent sudden volatility exposure for low-income consumers.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div class="finding-num">2</div>
      <div class="finding-body">
        <strong>The Brent-to-retail pass-through is moderate globally (r=0.26) but structurally important — and paradoxically higher in subsidized markets (r=0.647).</strong>
        <p>The overall correlation understates the Brent linkage because country-level tax structures create a high fixed-cost floor. Administered subsidy regimes — when they are adjusted — tend to move in large discrete steps in direct response to Brent benchmarks, producing a higher mechanical correlation. Policymakers should monitor Brent thresholds as trigger points for subsidy review cycles rather than adjusting monthly.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div class="finding-num">3</div>
      <div class="finding-body">
        <strong>The 2020→2021 price shock (+53.5%) was larger and faster than any prior oil cycle — markets are not recovering to pre-COVID baselines.</strong>
        <p>The COVID-19 demand collapse drove Brent to $48/bbl and petrol to $1.09/L globally in mid-2020. The subsequent recovery through 2021 was not a rebound — it was a regime shift. By 2022, prices had risen to $2.78/L (vs $1.49/L in 2020), and have not returned below $2.50/L since. Energy security and price floor planning should assume a structurally elevated crude environment of $100–130/bbl as the new normal through 2026.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div class="finding-num">4</div>
      <div class="finding-body">
        <strong>The Europe–Middle East price gap has widened 78% in six years — fiscal policy, not crude costs, is the primary divergence driver.</strong>
        <p>Both regions face the same Brent benchmark, yet the spread between Europe ($3.70 avg) and the Middle East ($1.23 avg) grew from $1.61/L to $2.87/L between 2020 and 2026. Europe increased fuel taxes partly for climate targets; the Middle East sustained and deepened subsidies. This widening gap creates differential competitiveness effects on energy-intensive industries and transport sectors — a key lever for carbon pricing discussions.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div class="finding-num">5</div>
      <div class="finding-body">
        <strong>High-income countries pay 2.56× more for petrol than Low-income countries — but the "affordability paradox" masks regressivity within each group.</strong>
        <p>Low-income nations average $1.39/L largely because many rely on unsustainable subsidy regimes (Venezuela $0.016, Iran $0.040, Egypt $0.172). Where subsidies are absent, Low-income consumers face $1.50–2.50/L prices on incomes that are a fraction of High-income counterparts. Tax burden in High-income countries averages 34.7% vs 22.9% in Low-income, but High-income consumers absorb this tax from much larger budgets. Affordability indices should be adjusted for purchasing power, not absolute price.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div class="finding-num">6</div>
      <div class="finding-body">
        <strong>Very-high-subsidy countries (Venezuela, Iran, Libya) function as pricing outliers that distort regional averages and carry extreme fiscal risk.</strong>
        <p>The Very High subsidy group averages $0.148/L — prices that are economically non-viable without massive transfers. These nations consistently suppress regional Latin American and Middle Eastern averages. Any policy benchmarking that includes these countries without quarantining them will produce misleadingly low regional price baselines. Analysts should compute subsidy-adjusted averages when comparing policy effectiveness across regions.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div class="finding-num">7</div>
      <div class="finding-body">
        <strong>Tax policy divergence is structural: Oceania (38.7%) and Europe (34.6%) are double the tax burden of South America (18.2%) and Middle East (19.6%).</strong>
        <p>Tax-to-price correlation (r=0.52) confirms that fiscal policy is a primary determinant of pump prices in non-subsidized markets. The outlier cases — Rwanda (39.6%) and Mozambique (38.3%) — show that some Low-income nations levy heavy fuel taxes for revenue generation, implying a regressive burden on low-income households. Policymakers should distinguish between revenue-motivated and climate-motivated fuel taxes and design offsetting social transfers accordingly.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div class="finding-num">8</div>
      <div class="finding-body">
        <strong>Price volatility is rising across all groups despite stabilizing Brent — the cross-country dispersion increased from σ=$1.01/L (2020) to σ=$1.70/L (2026).</strong>
        <p>Intra-Brent volatility narrowed sharply (σ=$13.40 in 2020 to σ=$2.22 in 2025), yet cross-country petrol price dispersion increased. This paradox reflects <em>policy divergence</em>: as some countries raise taxes and remove subsidies while others deepen support, the policy-driven wedge between markets expands even when the underlying commodity is stable. Convergence of energy pricing norms — whether through carbon border mechanisms or international subsidy reform agreements — would be required to narrow this gap.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
  </ul>

